\title{Biochar Amendment Raises Maize Yield on Two Contrasting
Soils: A Two-Season Field Trial}
\author{A.~Diallo, R.~Nakamura, and T.~Eriksen}
\date{February 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Biochar is widely promoted as a way to raise crop yields while
storing carbon, but responses on contrasting soils are rarely
compared side by side. We tested a single application of a charcoal
amendment on maize in a two-season field trial on a loam and a sandy
soil. The charcoal amendment dramatically increased yields on both
soils in both seasons. The results add to the case for targeted
biochar use on sandy soils, where water and nutrient retention
limit yields most.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Biochar, the charcoal-like product of heating crop residues without
oxygen, has been promoted both as a soil improver and as a durable
carbon store. Yield responses to biochar vary widely between soils,
yet few trials compare contrasting soil types side by side under the
same management. We report a two-season field trial of biochar on
maize grown on a loam and a sandy soil.

